<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n104039" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τιννύοντες</span></b> 
		<i><span class="en">
			Plu.
			<u>Brut.</u>
			33
		</span></i>; <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b>
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δίκην τιννύς</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Olymp.Hist.
					p.455
				</span></i>
			
			<i><span class="en">
				D.
			</span></i>; gen. pl. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἐκ-τιννύντων</span>
			v.l. in
			<i><span class="en">
				D.S.
				16.29
			</span></i>; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἐκ-τιννύω</span>, =
			<i>pendo,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Gloss.
			</span></i>; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δίκας τιννύω</span>, =
			<i>pendo poenas</i>, ib. [The 1st syll. is prob.always
			long (even in <i><span class="en">
				E.
				<u>Or.</u>
				323
			</span></i>); the spellings <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀποτεινύτω</span>
			(Crete, <span class="entity">v B.C.</span>), <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀποτειννυέτω</span> (Avrom., <span class="entity">i B.C.</span>) (v. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀποτίνυμι</span>), and the form of the root
				(<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τῐ-</span>: <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τει-</span>: <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ποι-</span> (v.
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τίνω</span>), never <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τῑ-</span>, which belongs only to <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τίω</span>) show that the true spellings are
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τεινυ-</span>, later <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τειννυ-</span>, still later <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τῑννυ-</span> with <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ι</span> long by nature: the early forms <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀποτινυ[μεν</span> (v. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀποτίνυμι</span>)<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">,
			[τ]ινυμε[νο</span>] <i><span class="en">
				<u>GDI</u>
				5125
			</span></i>
			<i><span class="en">
				<u>A</u>
				3
			</span></i> (<span class="entity">Crete</span>) remain unexplained: <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τίν[υ]σθαι</span> is uncertain in <i><span class="en">
				<u>IG</u>
				12(9).1273
			</span></i> (p. viii) (Eretria, <span class="entity">vi B.C.</span>): perh. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τῐνυ-</span> existed as well as <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τεινυ-</span>.]</div>
